{
	"$schema": "./node_modules/fallow/schema.json",

	// Nx/pnpm monorepo. Both globs are already in pnpm-workspace.yaml; fallow
	// discovers them on its own, but stating them keeps the scope explicit.
	"workspaces": {
		"patterns": ["apps/*", "packages/*"]
	},

	// Every runtime root the module graph cannot infer. The four apps are bundled
	// from `src/main`; `packages/*/src/index.ts` is named here so a package is a
	// root whether or not it has been built. That seeds the graph. What resolves
	// the edges *between* packages is the `fallow` export condition below.
	"entry": [
		"apps/*/src/main.{ts,tsx}",
		"packages/*/src/index.ts",

		// Standalone scripts invoked by package.json, not imported by anything.
		// `dev`, `check:role-ladder`, `seed:role-ladder`, `seed:sync-baseline`.
		"apps/server/src/dev/run-dev-server.ts",
		"apps/server/src/check-role-ladder.ts",
		"packages/db/src/seed-*.ts",
		"packages/auth/src/probe-reset-password.ts",

		// The basemap generator's two hand-run commands (`node
		// scripts/map-style/build.mjs`, `… contrast.mjs`). They have no
		// package.json script to be discovered from, and naming them here is what
		// makes `layers.mjs`, `palette.mjs`, and `preview.mjs` reachable — those
		// three are imports, not entry points, so they need nothing of their own.
		"scripts/map-style/build.mjs",
		"scripts/map-style/contrast.mjs",

		// Second published entry point of @simmer-mosquito/db, imported by the
		// server's integration suites via `@simmer-mosquito/db/test-support`.
		"packages/db/src/test-support/db-integration.ts",

		// Loaded by path from `.changeset/config.json`, which is data rather than
		// code, so nothing imports it and the graph cannot see the edge.
		".changeset/changelog-simmer.mjs"
	],

	// Vendored shadcn primitives: imported, but not ours to edit. Hidden rather
	// than excluded — `ignorePatterns` would drop them from the module graph and
	// take everything downstream of them with it.
	//
	// `routeTree.gen.ts` is deliberately absent. It is generated and biome skips
	// it, but fallow's TanStack Router plugin already resolves the route tree, so
	// a pattern for it suppresses nothing and fallow warns that it matched no
	// finding. Never move it to `ignorePatterns`: it is the single edge making
	// every file under `src/routes` reachable from `main.tsx`, and dropping it
	// would report all three SPAs' routes as dead.
	"ignoreFindings": ["packages/ui-web/src/components/ui/**"],

	// The duplication ratchet. CI runs `fallow dupes` with no flags, so this is
	// what makes it a gate: 9.0% is where the workspace sits, and 9.1 leaves a
	// tenth of a point of slack so an ordinary change does not fail on rounding.
	//
	// It was 9.2 against 9.1% before #108 gave the organization-settings choice
	// card one home — the same tinted frame had been written out in `adult.tsx`
	// and `larval.tsx` and then rebuilt a third time for density inference.
	//
	// It was 9.3 against 9.2% before #106 gave the two sign-in screens one set of
	// auth form parts and moved the session controller — which both apps had
	// character for character — into `@simmer-mosquito/auth/browser`.
	//
	// It was 11.1 against 10.1% before #101 took the page skeleton out of the
	// nine map explorers — the count line, the date-range wiring, the paged fetch
	// with its page clamping, the fly-to-selection effect, and the sticky header
	// they all sat in. That is 1,594 duplicated lines, the largest single drop so
	// far, and unlike the entry below it moves the ratio the way you would expect
	// because what came out was duplication rather than scaffolding.
	//
	// An earlier one went 11.0 → 11.1 rather than down, and the reason is the trap in
	// the paragraph below: `map-tiles.ts` gave up 324 lines of injection
	// boilerplate that was not duplicated, so the denominator shrank while the
	// numerator barely moved — 18,293 duplicated lines became 18,266. Absolute
	// duplication fell; the ratio rose. Do not read the bump as a regression, and
	// do not read the ratio as a measure of quality.
	//
	// It was 11.1 against 11.0% before the eleven map surfaces in packages/db
	// became declarations over one factory, and 13.7 against 12.6% before
	// #111–#113 pulled the command write primitives, the location-source resolver
	// and the endpoint write tail into one home each.
	//
	// Read this as "did the branch make it worse", not "lower is better". A large
	// clone group can be genuinely divergent code that merely shares a shape —
	// the two domain overview panels are the standing example, and collapsing
	// them would be wrong however good it looked here. Equally, a branch that
	// adds a lot of non-duplicated code lowers the ratio and could hide copies it
	// added; the number is a floor under regressions, not a measure of quality.
	//
	// Lower it when real duplication comes out (`pnpm fallow:baseline` re-reads
	// both this and the complexity baseline). Raising it needs a reason in the
	// commit message.
	// What makes this gate build-independent, and #334.
	//
	// A cross-package import resolves through the target's package.json
	// `exports`, and every one of those points at `dist/`. So in a checkout that
	// has never been built there is nothing behind them, and fallow reported
	// unresolved imports for every export subpath it could not see through.
	// Fully built it reported something else again, and CI — which runs this
	// gate six steps before `pnpm build` — reported a third thing. The gate is
	// meant to answer "did this branch make it worse" and cannot, if the answer
	// tracks what happens to be on disk.
	//
	// So every subpath whose `import` points at `dist/` carries a `fallow`
	// condition naming the source it is built from, and this reads it. Nothing
	// else does: Node, tsc, Vite and Metro all ignore a condition nobody asked
	// for, so what the apps run is unchanged.
	//
	// It has to be the **first** key of the export object. fallow honours `types`
	// as a built-in and takes the first key it matches, so a condition listed
	// after it resolves to a `.d.ts` under `dist/` that an unbuilt checkout does
	// not have — inert, while looking like it works.
	//
	// The name is deliberately the tool's rather than something like `source` or
	// `development`. fallow honours `development` with no config at all, and so
	// does Vite in dev — using it would have switched all three SPAs to
	// importing package source with nothing saying so.
	//
	// `pnpm check:build-graph` asserts every dist-pointing subpath has one, so a
	// new package cannot quietly reintroduce this.
	"resolve": {
		"conditions": ["fallow"]
	},

	"duplicates": {
		"threshold": 9.1
	},

	// `@tanstack/db` is a deliberate pin, not an import. `@tanstack/react-db` and
	// `@tanstack/electric-db-collection` each depend on an exact version of it, so
	// apps/web and packages/sync list it to keep all three moving together — a
	// bump that misses one of them is inert. Nothing imports it directly and
	// nothing should.
	//
	// `fallow-type-aware` is fallow's own semantic sidecar: a spawned binary, not
	// an import. It is a direct devDependency only so pnpm links its `.CMD` shim
	// into `node_modules/.bin`, which is what makes `--type-aware` runnable on
	// Windows — see scripts/fallow.mjs. Keep its version in lockstep with fallow.
	//
	// `@tanstack/react-query-devtools` is mounted ad hoc while debugging a query
	// rather than imported, so it reads as unused and is deliberately kept.
	//
	// The two React Native entries are the same category as `@tanstack/db` — a
	// version pin rather than an import. `react-native-gesture-handler` is a peer
	// of `expo-router` that the native navigators load themselves, and
	// `react-native-worklets` is a peer of `expo-modules-core`. `apps/mobile`
	// names both because `auto-install-peers` otherwise resolves them to the
	// newest published version, which for both was already outside the range the
	// SDK asks for. Nothing in `src` imports either, and nothing should.
	"ignoreDependencies": [
		"@tanstack/db",
		"@tanstack/react-query-devtools",
		"fallow-type-aware",
		"react-native-gesture-handler",
		"react-native-worklets"
	],

	"rules": {
		// Opt-in API-hygiene check. The packages expose their public surface
		// through top-level barrel modules (CLAUDE.md), so an exported signature
		// that references a file-private type is a real break for the apps
		// consuming it. Advisory for now.
		"private-type-leaks": "warn"
	},

	"overrides": [
		{
			// The apps are leaves: nothing imports them, so an unexported type in
			// one of their signatures cannot break a consumer. The check earns its
			// keep at the package seams, and only there.
			"files": ["apps/**"],
			"rules": { "private-type-leaks": "off" }
		},
		{
			// Kysely table definitions. The flagged names (`TimestampWithDefault`,
			// `GeometryColumn`, `GeneratedColumn`, …) are local notation for
			// describing a column, repeated across ~180 field declarations — they
			// describe the schema rather than form an API anyone needs to name.
			"files": ["packages/db/src/tables.ts"],
			"rules": { "private-type-leaks": "off" }
		}
	]
}
